# No context text on the website

Member-facing UI must not show **meta commentary** about how the site is built, how data is fetched, or what engineers decided. That reads as unfinished and unprofessional.

## Hard rule

Do **not** put on the page (body copy, muted footnotes, card subtitles, empty-state asides):

- How a link or list is sourced ("polled from GitHub", "listing is static", "baked at build time", "Edge Function", "RPC")
- Implementation status ("not wired yet", "temporary", "placeholder", "for now")
- Dev/process asides ("we always track the latest release", "this tab was moved from a modal")
- Internal product nicknames next to the display name when the tab already shows the product name

Members need **what the product is** and **what to click**. How we keep it up to date belongs in Archive, README, or code comments — not under the download button.

## Allowed on the page

- What the tool does in plain language
- Requirements the member must meet (sign-in, RSI verified, Chromium browser)
- Errors and empty states about *their* action ("Could not load downloads — try again")
- Legal / affiliation one-liners when required (e.g. unofficial fan tool, not CIG)
- Trust/verify material members use (VirusTotal findings, checksum verify links) when that is the product surface

## Prefer

- Delete the muted `text-xs text-slate-500` footnote if it only explains implementation
- Put control how-to in `SiteTooltip` (see `site-tooltips.mdc`), not a paragraph under the control
- Keep engineer notes in code comments or `docs/`

## Bad vs good

```tsx
// BAD — meta about our pipeline
<p className="text-xs text-slate-500">
  Listing is static (not polled from GitHub).
</p>

// GOOD — nothing, or a real requirement
<p className="text-sm text-slate-400">
  Add RSI+ from the Chrome Web Store. Works in Chrome or Chromium-based Edge.
</p>
```
